{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Investigation update — the epic-filter framing is REFUTED; correcting two claims in the original report\n\n**New observation (from the operator):** the identical failure occurs on a *different* tool that takes no\nstatus/type filter at all:\n\n```\n○ ready_tickets (MCP: rebar)\n✗ Execution failed: TypeError: Do not know how to serialize a BigInt\n```\n\nThis falsifies any hypothesis that depends on the `ticket_type=\"epic\"` filter, on `status=\"open\"`, or on\n`list_tickets`' filtering path specifically. Recording it as a falsification result, not a detail.\n\n### Correction 1 — the report's \"Likely cause\" section is wrong about *where* the failure happens\n\nThe filed report says: *\"Some numeric field ... is represented as a JS `BigInt` somewhere in **the MCP\nserver's serialization path**\"*. That is incorrect. This ticket was authored by GitHub Copilot CLI (the\nclient that hit the error), and it attributed a JavaScript error to a Python server's serializer. rebar's\nMCP server is Python; it has no `BigInt` type and no `JSON.stringify`. The throw is in the JS client.\n\nWhat rebar *does* do is emit integers outside JavaScript's safe range as **bare JSON numbers**. Measured\nfrom the real JSON-RPC bytes emitted by `python -m rebar.mcp_server` over stdio (`REBAR_MCP_READONLY=1`):\n\n| tool | unsafe ints (\\|n\\| > 2^53-1) | fields |\n|---|---|---|\n| `list_tickets(status=open, ticket_type=epic)` | 14 | `created_at` ×7, `updated_at` ×7 |\n| `ready_tickets()` | 144 | `created_at` ×52, `updated_at` ×52, `timestamp` ×34, `signed_at` ×6 |\n| `show_ticket()` | 2 | `created_at`, `updated_at` |\n| `search()` | 0 | — |\n| `summary()` | 0 | — |\n| `ticket_deps()` / `explain_criterion()` / `grounding_info()` | 0 | — |\n\nThe pass/fail split is *predicted* by which response models carry ticket timestamps, and it matches the\ntwo observed outcomes exactly: the two tools reported failing are in the top group; the tools with no\nticket-record timestamps are clean.\n\n### Correction 2 — this is not a total failure of the MCP server\n\n`mcp__rebar__list_tickets` **succeeded** from Claude Code earlier in the same session (219KB result). Any\nhypothesis predicting universal failure is wrong. Confirmed on Node v22.23.2:\n\n```\nJSON.parse('{\"created_at\":1786649271159032001}').created_at\n  -> 1786649271159032000        (a Number — SILENTLY LOSES the last 3 digits)\nJSON.stringify({x: BigInt(\"1786649271159032001\")})\n  -> TypeError: Do not know how to serialize a BigInt     (exact match for the reported string)\n```\n\nSo a client using plain `JSON.parse` succeeds while silently corrupting the timestamp; a client using a\nBigInt-preserving parser gets a `BigInt` and throws on re-stringify. One cause, both outcomes.\n\n### On the ~30–80s latency\n\nConsistent with failing *after* the server did its work: `JSON.stringify` runs on an already-parsed\nresult. Driving the same call locally against a warm store took 3.7s, so the latency is store/IO work,\nnot the serialization step. Weak evidence, recorded for completeness.\n\nAcceptance criterion 1 (\"which field, and why it's a BigInt rather than a Number/string\") is answered:\nthe fields are `created_at`, `updated_at`, `timestamp`, `signed_at` — `time.time_ns()` nanosecond\nintegers, 19 digits — and they are *not* BigInts in rebar; they become BigInts in the client because\nrebar puts them on the wire as bare JSON numbers outside the RFC 8259 §6 interoperable range.\n\nRoot-cause confirmation and the defect/not-a-bug judgment follow in a subsequent comment."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787856976224609473,"uuid":"d59f93e7-779b-45ff-a771-4f90975157d0"}